{
  "arguments": [
    {
      "claim": "The captured member of the Tarsk Highland Volunteers does not qualify as a prisoner of war under Article 4(2) of the Third Geneva Convention because the militia fails to meet condition (b) requiring a fixed distinctive sign recognizable at a distance.",
      "reasoning": "Article 4(2) lists the criteria that a militia must satisfy to be entitled to POW status. The Tarsk Highland Volunteers are commanded by a responsible officer and carry arms openly, satisfying conditions (a) and (c), and they conduct operations in accordance with the laws and customs of war, satisfying (d). However, the commander’s written instruction that members wear ordinary civilian clothing with no insignia means they lack a fixed distinctive sign recognizable at a distance, breaching condition (b). Because all four conditions are cumulative, the failure of (b) excludes the captured combatant from the POW category defined in Article 4(2).",
      "citations": [
        {
          "type": "treaty",
          "instrument": "gc_iii",
          "article": "4",
          "quoted_text": "that of having a fixed distinctive sign recognizable at a distance"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Regardless of the failure to meet the distinctive‑sign requirement, the captured militia member must be treated as a prisoner of war at least provisionally under Article 45(1) of Additional Protocol I, which reflects a customary rule of presumption of POW status for anyone who takes part in hostilities and falls into the power of an adverse Party.",
      "reasoning": "Article 45(1) of AP I provides that a person who takes part in hostilities and is captured is presumed to be a POW until a competent tribunal determines otherwise. This presumption applies even when there is doubt about the applicability of Article 4(2). Both Ardovia and Tarsk are parties to the 1949 Conventions, and the presumption in AP I is widely regarded as reflecting customary international humanitarian law, binding on all parties to the Conventions. Consequently, the captured volunteer must be afforded POW protection pending a proper status determination, and Ardovia’s refusal to do so breaches this presumption and the corresponding customary rule.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "type": "treaty",
          "instrument": "ap_i",
          "article": "45(1)",
          "quoted_text": "A person who takes part in hostilities and falls into the power of an adverse Party shall be presumed to be a prisoner of war, and therefore shall be protected by the Third Convention, if he claims the status of prisoner of war, or if he appears to be entitled to such status, or if the Party on which he depends claims such status on his behalf by notification to the detaining Power or to the Protecting Power. Should any doubt arise as to whether any such person is entitled to the status of prisoner of war, he shall continue to have such status and, therefore, to be protected by the Third Convention and this Protocol until such time as his status has been determined by a competent tribunal."
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
